description:
RFC 8620 §5.3 paragraph 5: 'Records may have any properties set on them; however, the server SHOULD reject the create with an invalidProperties SetError if any property cannot be set or has an invalid value. […] The id property MUST NOT be set in the create object.' (paraphrasing — the spec wording is that id is server-set and any client-provided value is invalid).

src/calendar.rs:88-96, src/event.rs:144-151, src/participant_identity.rs:73-81 all use the same pattern:

    let obj_with_id = match obj_val {
        Value::Object(mut m) => {
            m.entry("id")
                .or_insert_with(|| Value::String("placeholder".to_owned()));
            Value::Object(m)
        }
        other => other,
    };

This injects a placeholder "id" only if the client did NOT supply one. If the client DID supply "id": "client-chosen-id", the placeholder injection is a no-op and the deserialiser accepts the client's id. The handler then proceeds to call create_object with the client's id baked into the object body — backends that trust the deserialised struct will store the client-chosen id.

Fix: invert the check. If the create payload contains a non-null "id" key, reject with invalidProperties citing properties:["id"]. The placeholder injection is needed only because Calendar/CalendarEvent/ParticipantIdentity have a non-Optional id field — that's a workaround, not a feature. The cleaner fix is to make Id optional during deserialisation of the create payload, but that's a types-crate change; the local fix is to validate in the handler before placeholder injection.

Test gap: no test sends "id" in a create entry to verify it's rejected. Add one per type.

This is a wire-protocol bug, not just an idiom issue: the server is accepting input it MUST reject per the base spec. A second client racing to create an object with the same id could confuse a backend that doesn't dedupe.
